      {
        anatomy.map((item) => (
          <div class="card p-5">
            <h3 class="text-base">{item.title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink/75" set:html={item.body} />
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <div class="prose-wp mx-auto mt-10 max-w-3xl">
      <h3>The output dose: why 1.0 mL ≠ 1.2 mL matters</h3>
      <p>
        Cosmetic formulators specify dose down to 0.1 mL because viscosity, fragrance load and
        skin-feel are calibrated against that volume. A pump that delivers 1.2 mL when speced at
        1.0 mL means each consumer use over-delivers by 20% — the bottle empties 20% faster, your
        repurchase cycle moves forward, and your cost-per-use maths breaks. Reputable factories test
        dose-per-stroke on at least 5 pumps per production batch and reject lots above ±0.05 mL
        deviation.
      </p>

      <h2>Lotion pump sizes: 24/410, 28/410, 28/412 decoded</h2>
      <p>
        The number on every lotion pump spec — 24/410, 28/410, 28/412 — is the neck finish code. The
        first number is the inside diameter of the bottle neck in millimetres; the second number
        indicates the thread style. Get this wrong and your pump won't seat — or will leak.
      </p>
